From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:07:37 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: qcom: Pass a wakeup from the PHYs on to the root
 hubs

With runtime PM enabled for the controller and for xHCI, a SuperSpeed
device that attaches while the controller is runtime-suspended is not
found. The wakeup interrupt of the SuperSpeed PHY is raised when the PHY
detects the device's receiver, before the link is trained. The handler
resumes the xHCI device; xhci_resume() resumes the root hubs only for a
port event that is already pending, finds none, and with nothing holding
it the xHCI device suspends again straight away. The link never comes
up and the device waits until something else resumes the root hub.

Seen on a Surface Laptop 8 with an NVMe enclosure that drops from USB4
to USB 3 without being plugged in again, so that no Type-C event starts
the host anew: with the controller suspended the drive did not appear
for 54 s, and appeared at SuperSpeed Plus within a second of the
SuperSpeed root hub being resumed through sysfs.

A wakeup from the PHYs is a wakeup request from a root hub port, so
pass it on with usb_hcd_resume_root_hub(), as a host controller driver
does for its own port events. The hub driver holds a resumed SuperSpeed
root hub awake for 120 ms, and xHCI holds a bus suspend off, for a
bounded time, while one of its ports is polling.

The handler does both with the mutex held that the role changes under: a
role change that waited for the controller to resume would otherwise take
the host away between the two.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c | 46 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 43 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
index 1d433c543..d3bb5b472 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
@@ -428,6 +428,37 @@ static int dwc3_qcom_resume(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom, bool wakeup)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The PHYs signal a wakeup when they see a device on the lines, which is
+ * before the port has anything to report: a SuperSpeed link still has to be
+ * trained. xHCI resumes its root hubs only for a port event that is already
+ * pending, finds none and suspends again, and the device is never seen.
+ * Pass the wakeup request on to the root hubs, as a host controller does for
+ * a wakeup from one of its ports. The hub driver keeps a resumed SuperSpeed
+ * root hub awake for 120 ms, and xHCI holds a bus suspend off for a port that
+ * is polling, for up to ten polling periods. A port that is still detecting
+ * the receiver after that gets the next wakeup, at the next suspend.
+ *
+ * The caller holds the mutex the role changes under.
+ */
+static void dwc3_qcom_resume_root_hubs(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom)
+{
+	struct usb_hcd __maybe_unused *hcd;
+
+	/*
+	 * FIXME: Fix this layering violation.
+	 */
+	hcd = platform_get_drvdata(qcom->dwc.xhci);
+	if (!hcd)
+		return;
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_USB
+	if (hcd->shared_hcd)
+		usb_hcd_resume_root_hub(hcd->shared_hcd);
+	usb_hcd_resume_root_hub(hcd);
+#endif
+}
+
 static irqreturn_t qcom_dwc3_resume_irq(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct dwc3_qcom *qcom = data;
@@ -438,11 +469,20 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_dwc3_resume_irq(int irq, void *data)
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 
 	/*
-	 * This is safe as role switching is done from a freezable workqueue
-	 * and the wakeup interrupts are disabled as part of resume.
+	 * A role change that waits for the controller to resume takes the
+	 * host away as soon as the resume below lets it run. Hold the mutex
+	 * the role changes under, so that the host stays until its root hubs
+	 * have been told. The wakeup interrupts are disabled without waiting
+	 * for this handler when the controller resumes, so a role change,
+	 * which resumes the controller with the mutex held, does not wait
+	 * for the handler in turn.
 	 */
-	if (dwc3_qcom_is_host(qcom))
+	mutex_lock(&dwc->mutex);
+	if (dwc3_qcom_is_host(qcom)) {
 		pm_runtime_resume(&dwc->xhci->dev);
+		dwc3_qcom_resume_root_hubs(qcom);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&dwc->mutex);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
-- 
2.56.1

